WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.276 --> 00:00:06.510
MATT GOODRIDGE: OK, good
afternoon everybody.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.160
My name's Matt Goodridge.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.360
I'm a product manager on the
Managed Google Play Store.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.590
That's Google's enterprise
app management platform

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.480
and app marketplace.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.790
So hopefully it's
not too much longer

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.910
before you guys get some beers.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.060
But for now, you're
stuck with me.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.280
So a quick show of hands,
how many developers have we

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.090
got in the room?

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:31.850
Awesome.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.016
Well, we're going to have
some code samples for you,

00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.960
so hopefully that will
keep you entertained.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.730
So whether you're an ISV, so
an independent software vendor,

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.810
or an enterprise developer
or a development agency,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.650
this presentation aims to give
you some tools and techniques

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.080
to make the best out of
your Android development

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.720
in the enterprise.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.980
First of all though, why
are we investing in mobile?

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.910
Well, mobile is now
becoming a significant part

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:06.960
of most enterprises'
digital strategies.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.090
And let's actually look at
some data as to why is mobile

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.830
so important.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:17.810
Well, first of all, it's about
increasing sales and revenue.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.530
It's about improving
employee productivity,

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.990
and then decreasing costs.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.070
And there's many other
reasons for it as well,

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.350
but those are really
some of the top ones.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.670
Then in terms of
Android, Android

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.180
is the biggest
operating system in

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.350
mobile across smartphone
devices today.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.780
And that extends
into enterprise.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:47.210
68% of mobile devices shipped
through an enterprise channel

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.850
were Android last year.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.110
And Android has a huge range
of devices that it works on,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:02.350
so everything from a smartwatch
to smartphones, tablets.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.650
And then recently
last year we've

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.800
announced Android apps
running on Chrome OS.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.660
So you've now got Android
apps running on laptop devices

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.880
and on large screen devices.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.530
So Chrome OS version 53 and
beyond on selected models,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.160
and then all Chrome OS devices
launched from this year onwards

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.960
will be able to
support Android apps.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.710
So if you're thinking about
developing on Android,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.180
this is a really good time.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.680
So let's also have a look at
the nature of mobile apps.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:43.820
Well, although hybrid-type apps
and web-type apps on mobile

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.340
are inherently
cross-platform, actually

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.110
what we see in
enterprises is they

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.920
continue to develop
on the native platform

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.600
itself, even though most
enterprises are cross-platform

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.480
in their nature.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.630
So they have employees
that are using

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.420
iOS as well as Android devices.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.270
So we are really going to focus
here on native Android app

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:07.650
development.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.710
And then in terms of how do
those Android apps actually

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.930
interact with other
services, well,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.600
when we surveyed
developers, we found

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.840
that the vast majority, well
over 90% of Android apps,

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.090
are actually part of a
bigger software solution.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.680
So they are communicating
with a cloud back end.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.280
So we're going to assume for the
purposes of this presentation

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.290
that that's going to
be the architecture.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.310
So that's just structure
this session briefly.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.105
So we're going to talk
in three sections.

00:03:42.105 --> 00:03:43.480
First of all we're
going to focus

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.890
on some aspects of design.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.810
Then we're going to talk about
building Android apps, and then

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:54.320
finally deploying those
apps out to enterprises.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.760
We're going to talk about
some critical best practices.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:04.180
And a lot of these have got
much more detailed documentation

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.620
available on the web.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.630
I'm going to show
a bunch of QR codes

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:09.460
in the corners of the slides.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.310
You can then use those to
look up that documentation.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.470
Some of them are
things like video links

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.701
which you can go and have
a look at when you're back

00:04:16.701 --> 00:04:17.284
in the office.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.160
So first of all, let's
talk about design.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.870
Why does it matter you
spend time on design?

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:31.150
From an enterprise
perspective, it's

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.622
about cost of your operations.

00:04:33.622 --> 00:04:36.700
Huh, what you mean cost?

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:44.900
Well, we've seen that mobile is
about increasing productivity.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.560
And if that's the main reason
that you're rolling out

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.010
your apps and
services to mobile,

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.380
then there's a few things
that we need to think about.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.050
Those apps need to be
quick for the employees

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.710
to use, which means
the lower time cost.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.950
They've got to be easy to use
as well so that you don't need

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:06.550
to have support
or lower support,

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.800
and minimal training required--

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.530
again, lower time cost.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.520
So if you don't carefully
invest in design

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.500
when you're building
your app, you

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.490
can actually risk missing out
on some of the key benefits

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.230
that you set out to
when you originally

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.140
decided to go mobile.

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.570
So how can mobile really
benefit your users?

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.880
We're going to
focus, first of all,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.350
on how you can use all of the
sensors and peripherals that

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:41.780
are built into modern
mobile devices.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.880
Because those are really going
to help your users quickly

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.490
get information into
apps rather than having

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.550
to type everything manually.

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:56.870
So mobile devices have a wealth
of sensors and peripherals.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:00.740
You've got cellular
connectivity as well as Wi-Fi.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:03.410
You've got GPS location.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.740
You've got motion sensors
like a gyro and accelerometer.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:11.810
You've got proximity
NFC for swiping badges,

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.620
Bluetooth for local
connectivity, but also

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:19.340
potentially indoor location
using Bluetooth beacons,

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:24.720
cameras for QR codes,
augmented reality, microphones

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.070
for things like
voice recognition,

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:31.080
a flashlight or torch, depending
on which side of the pond

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.550
you're from, for providing
light and then fingerprints,

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.300
which make your security
workflows, your sign

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.560
in workflow much slicker.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.510
So let's just look
at a few examples

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:49.590
to illustrate some
opportunities here.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.690
Sometimes it's some really
very simple things that help.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.180
In this example,
British Gas have

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:00.900
added just a light function
to their meter reading app.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.925
What they realized
was gas meters

00:07:02.925 --> 00:07:05.160
are often in dark places.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.980
And having that
light quickly to hand

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.960
when they're entering in
the meter reading actually

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.900
made it much quicker
to read a meter.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.060
So let's just look
quickly at the code here.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:23.040
So the light on a
smartphone is actually

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.110
part of the camera module.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:29.160
And there are multiple camera
modules on many smart devices.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.620
So you need to then
iterate through those

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.060
and identify, well, which is the
camera module that the light is

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:39.000
associated with, and
then switch that on,

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.850
and then returning the state
back to the calling method

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:47.560
so it knows that I've found
the light to switch on.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.130
You could also be a little bit
more ambitious with something

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.050
like augmented reality.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:55.380
So here's an example
from the marine industry,

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:00.090
helping mariners add
important information

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:03.540
about local shipping.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.170
So to get started with
augmenting reality,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:11.790
one of the things that you
need is a fixed reference point

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:15.750
in order to match the image
overlay with the image

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.060
that you're getting
from the camera.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:22.030
There's a really, really
good reference point,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.340
which is gravity.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.730
Gravity is always pointing down.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:30.930
And conveniently,
Android provides a sensor

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.890
for the gravity vector.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:37.200
So you can look at or
you can get the gravity

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.470
sensor from the sensor
service within Android,

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.470
create a callback
method which then,

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:47.520
for each update of that gravity
vector that the sensor is

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.800
taking, you can
extract that vector

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:54.930
and then use that to update
your overlay over the image

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.640
that your camera is taking.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:03.450
And then of course we need to
enable that callback method

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.470
by registering it as a listener
for updates from the gravity

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:09.952
sensor.

00:09:09.952 --> 00:09:11.410
And then finally
I want to give you

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.620
an example of an
internal app from Google

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.670
called Google Fixit.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:19.120
Fixit is used by
Googlers in order

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.180
to report problems
with our building

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:27.580
facilities and IT equipment
that's around our buildings.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.770
As you can see from
this screenshot,

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.670
before I've even
entered a location,

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:36.220
it already knows that
I'm on the 10th floor

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.170
of the building I was in
when I took the screenshot.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.940
And when I then start
to enter the exact room,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.779
it will autocomplete for me.

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:48.570
So there's three things
that we really need

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.000
to get that kind of experience.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:54.810
Number one is get the location.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.590
Secondly is to look up against
a series of points of interest

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.920
that might be interesting
in your application,

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.380
and then of course
do the autocomplete.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.050
I'm going to focus specifically
on the location piece

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.680
because that's the mobile bit.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.950
The rest you'll probably
already be doing in the cloud.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:17.940
So on the location side, there's
really two techniques for this.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:23.190
Android already keeps a
regular poll of your location.

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:28.360
So you can just look up the last
known location of the device.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.630
But that's sometimes
not entirely accurate

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.990
because it only does that with
a relatively low frequency

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.390
in order to conserve
battery life.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.970
But it might be useful
if you know your user has

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.610
been stationary for a while.

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.010
It's also very quick because
that location is already

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:46.630
stored in memory.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:49.980
The second approach
is then to start

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.270
listening for location updates.

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:57.060
So this is then activating a
particular location provider.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:00.180
In this example, I've got GPS.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:01.710
And as you probably
know, it takes

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:05.040
a little while for a GPS
chipset to actually acquire

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.410
a good fix of satellites.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.820
So you can start
sampling location updates

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.190
and then provide
those location updates

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:19.560
to a short method, which can
then determine for you which

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.900
is the most accurate take.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:25.110
So for this particular
example, one way

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:29.640
to get a combination of accuracy
and a good user experience

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.400
is as soon as the user first
clicks into the autocompleting

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:41.520
text field is to first call the
get last known location method.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.110
So you can get
something quickly,

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:46.260
responsively for the
user to start typing,

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:53.100
but at the same time start
sampling for a new, more

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:54.810
accurate location.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.070
And as your user
is typing, you're

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:59.850
anyway updating your
autocomplete list.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:04.110
You can also be using an
updated location in parallel

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.930
in order to refine that list,
and therefore minimizing

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:09.990
the amount of time that
your user needs to spend

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:14.120
when entering this information.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.970
So we're going to change
gears now and talk

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:20.560
about some techniques
for building your app.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.720
The first one, of
course, is security.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.920
So I could probably spend
most of today talking

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.570
about security on Android.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.150
And there has already
been a number of talks

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.640
about that this week.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:38.190
But let's just structure this,
and then we can focus down

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:40.260
on a particular area.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.380
So I've got three
areas here-- first

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.660
of all, platform security.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:49.650
We harden the Android platform
against direct attack.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:54.480
Ecosystem security-- we're
providing security updates

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:57.400
for device OEMs.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:01.170
We're also putting in
a lot of app scanning

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.500
and other tools
within Google Play

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.760
to make sure our users are
confident that apps that they

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:09.960
are installing are safe.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:15.270
And then finally, the apps
themselves, your apps,

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.750
need to be themselves
free from vulnerabilities.

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:22.800
So Adrian Ludwig, our
security director for Android,

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:23.860
spoke yesterday.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.430
And he really focused
on numbers one and two.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.770
So I'm going to focus
here on some techniques

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:30.870
that you should think
about in number three,

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.630
really making sure
that your apps are

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:36.700
free from vulnerabilities.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:38.890
So the top three things--

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.290
if there's just three
things you think

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:44.140
about when securing
your app, these

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.120
are the three that
we recommend--

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:50.320
firstly, making sure you're
using only secure network

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:55.020
protocols; secondly, using the
default local storage that's

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.640
provided by Android; and
then thirdly, checking that

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.210
the device is authentic
and uncompromised

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.770
when you're running on it.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.140
There's a full list
actually at this QR code

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:10.620
here with extensive
details about how

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.200
to further secure your apps.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.030
Let's just go into a
few more details though.

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:20.990
It might be stating the obvious,
but we see this time and time

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.300
again that apps are
still communicating

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.290
to their back ends
in clear text.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:29.110
So let's just reiterate this.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:33.550
Securing and encrypting
your app's communications

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:37.630
to its back end is absolutely
critical in protecting data

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:39.400
in transit.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.650
The most effective
way of doing this

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:45.250
is to tell Android to stop
your app from actually

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:47.290
using clear text protocols.

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:49.690
And you can do that in
your app's manifest.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:53.860
You can simply set up a
network security configuration

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:59.802
to ensure that cleartext
traffic is not permitted.

00:14:59.802 --> 00:15:01.260
This is particularly
important when

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:03.900
you're using third party SDKs.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:06.870
You can set up development
processes internally

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:11.550
to make sure that you
are yourselves writing

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:14.530
SSL-based communications.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:17.280
But if you're using third
party SDKs that might also

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.700
be communicating to
some back end, enforcing

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.190
this here will mean
that those SDKs also

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.550
can't use cleartext protocols.

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:28.900
If you're using
Firebase for your app,

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:31.890
then that already uses
secure protocols back

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:35.930
to Google's back end.

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:39.770
While on the subject
of secure protocols,

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:43.070
you should also be aware
that many enterprises

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:50.450
use content proxying as another
measure to secure their data.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:53.120
The way that works is
that the enterprise

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:57.350
would deploy a SSL certificate,
a custom certificate,

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:02.420
on the device, and use
that for the SSL encryption

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:03.660
to the proxy.

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:05.630
And then the proxy back
to the service back end

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:10.480
would then use the
service's own certificate.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:12.790
So in that case,
you're effectively

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:17.430
man in the middling
your own app.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:25.350
Since Android Nougat, we've
made some security changes such

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:29.580
that Android actually ignores
those custom certificates

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:35.520
and lets an app
explicitly opt in.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:38.040
So here you have, in
the manifest then,

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:41.040
a way to actually
opt in for that.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.860
If you're a development
agency or you're

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.350
an internal
enterprise developer,

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.930
then actually finding
the list of domains

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:52.290
that you need to whitelist
is probably straightforward,

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:54.390
and that list is
relatively small.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.990
So you can set up a
whitelist a lot like this.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:04.140
If you're an ISV and potentially
using or distributing your app

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:07.140
to many customers, then
that list of domains

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:08.730
is going to be very, very long.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:14.640
So you should consider
enabling that for all domains.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:18.276
And you can set that
as a base configuration

00:17:18.276 --> 00:17:21.510
within your network
security policy.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:25.140
Obviously that opens
things up a little bit.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:27.589
So the way the
enterprise can then

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:33.780
resecure that to make sure
that we haven't introduced

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:37.080
any additional
security holes there

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.270
is to use device
management where

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:42.372
they can restrict and manage
which certificates are actually

00:17:42.372 --> 00:17:43.330
deployed to the device.

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:51.400
So let's move on now to
data storage on the device

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:55.600
so Android uses a sandbox
execution environment such

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:58.030
that all of an
app's data is only

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:03.150
available to that app itself
unless it's explicitly shared.

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:06.400
Also from Marshmallow
onwards, we've

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:10.390
required the device
storage encryption

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:13.210
to be implemented as standard.

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:16.840
And if the device is managed by
an enterprise mobility manager

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:19.720
or device management
system, then that actually

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:23.270
might being required by policy.

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:27.020
Also in Android
Nougat and beyond,

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:30.320
we can also use
separate encryption keys

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:34.100
for work data versus
personal data in storage.

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:35.840
And we're going
to talk about data

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.420
separation in a little bit.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:42.630
On external storage
then, external storage

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.640
is globally readable
and writable.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.160
It's not part of that sandbox
execution environment.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:52.020
So you really shouldn't store
any sensitive information

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:54.060
in that area.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:58.020
Also an administrator might
set policies to actually block

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.460
the use of external storage.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:02.880
So you should certainly
have a fallback position

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:06.170
if you do need to use that.

00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:08.220
Also if you do
need to use it, do

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:11.580
make sure you perform input
validation when reading back

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:14.310
from that external
storage as you

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:17.400
would for any other
untrusted source of data.

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:24.350
And then finally,
how do you know

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:27.020
that the device that
your app is running on

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:30.170
is not vulnerable
or compromised?

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:32.780
Well, the SafetyNet
Attest API, which

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:36.500
is part of Google's
mobile services,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:39.050
provides that capability.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:42.800
It examines the software
and hardware information

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:46.890
about the device that
you're running on,

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:50.030
and it attempts to match
that software and hardware

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:54.210
fingerprint against a
set of known fingerprints

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:57.440
that Google maintains.

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:00.910
It does basic device
integrity checking,

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:06.950
and it also can check details
about the APK, your app, that's

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:08.910
calling the API as well.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:11.360
And then you can send
that bundle of data

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:16.340
back to your server to interpret
to make sure not only is

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.910
the device uncompromised,
but the app

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:23.720
that's calling your
server is itself the app

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:24.920
that you expect it to be.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.900
You might have caught
Ed Cunningham's talk

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:35.960
earlier this afternoon,
which is Google Security

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:38.300
Services for Android.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:40.910
If you didn't get a
chance to see that,

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:42.370
then do catch the video.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.080
He goes into a lot more
detail about how this works

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:46.830
and how you can implement it.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:48.860
Also there's more
details at this QR code.

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:54.470
So let's move on then
to single sign-on.

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:57.260
Many, many companies
use single sign-on

00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:01.220
to enable their users to use a
single user name and password

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:03.830
for many software services.

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:08.000
It's been a feature of web-based
services for a long time,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.720
but getting a slick experience
within a native mobile app

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:14.840
has been pretty
challenging until now.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.600
So here we're
assuming that there's

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:21.020
an enterprise authentication
server in the back end,

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:25.450
and you're potentially using a
third party identity provider.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:31.320
So today, a user first of all
needs to enter an identifier

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:33.030
when they log into this app.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:35.340
Particularly if
this is an ISV app,

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.350
the app itself
doesn't necessarily

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:42.990
know yet which identity provider
it's going to go back to.

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.180
So when they enter
their username,

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:48.940
the app and the service
back end is then

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:53.640
going to use that to determine
which identity provider to make

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.410
the authorization call back to.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.570
Then once that's done,
the identity provider

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:02.670
will present the login screen.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:05.970
The user needs to enter
their username and password,

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:06.960
and they're in.

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:09.240
But then they have to
do the same for one

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:16.410
for each individual app
that uses those credentials.

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.890
Wouldn't it be great if
mobile SSO could actually work

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:22.710
the same as it does on the web?

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:26.820
Well, the good news is it can.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.450
And once you've got
an established login

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:33.100
session for each app that
the user starts using,

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.570
they can go straight in.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:37.970
So there's two steps to that.

00:22:37.970 --> 00:22:41.510
First of all, we've
got to determine

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:45.140
which SSO provider to use.

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:48.590
And then we're going to use
a feature of Chrome, Chrome

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:53.240
Custom Tabs, in order to
persist that login state,

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:56.480
and so that all apps which
are using the same technique

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.160
can share that login state.

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:05.320
So here's quickly how that
identity provider resolution

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:06.810
works.

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:11.950
So we're going to start off with
an enterprise mobility manager.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:13.900
So the device is
under management.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:18.640
That's going to send a
login hint field down

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:19.990
to the Android app.

00:23:19.990 --> 00:23:22.150
Now this should start to
sound familiar if you've

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.490
implemented SSO on the web.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:27.600
We're going to talk
about exactly how you

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:29.910
get that configuration
down to the mobile app

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.170
a little bit later.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:34.410
So the Android app's
now got this login hint.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.830
This is probably
the user's username.

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:42.300
The app can then send that
down to the SaaS back end

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:44.880
and then onwards
to the auth server

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:50.540
and determine which is the
identity provider to use.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:55.010
The identity provider will then
present the login screen up

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:56.220
to the user.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:58.170
Now if you configure
the auth server

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:01.750
to pass that login hint directly
into the username field,

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.000
then that username field
can already be prepopulated

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:07.995
for the user, and all they have
to do is enter their password.

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:16.420
So then let's talk about how do
we persist that login session.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:19.080
So we're going to use
Chrome Custom Tabs.

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:23.380
But let's first talk about what
exactly is a Chrome Custom Tab.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:27.820
It's a secure context, so
that it can load any web page,

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:32.860
but that host app can't
actually inspect its contents.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:34.780
Users can see the
server address,

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:36.480
but they can't edit it.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.910
And the cookie state is
shared between any instance

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:43.270
of a Chrome Custom Tab
and indeed Chrome itself.

00:24:43.270 --> 00:24:46.840
So that's how we're going to
get our persistent login state.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:49.300
It's a system browser
activity, so you

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:51.730
don't need to worry
about a user having

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:55.950
to navigate to another activity
and then come back again.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:25:00.040
It's been built in to
Chrome since version 45.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:01.570
So it's been around
a little while

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.600
and is almost guaranteed
to be on the Chrome version

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:08.470
that you're running on.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:13.240
And it also works from
Android Jelly Bean and beyond.

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:15.736
In this example, I show
you Google accounts,

00:25:15.736 --> 00:25:17.110
but you don't
actually need to be

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.670
using Google accounts for this.

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:22.180
This will work with any
supported identity provider,

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:28.040
whether they're using
OAuth or OpenID Connect.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.655
So the good news is
the OpenID Foundation

00:25:31.655 --> 00:25:35.690
have put together a library
called the AppAuth library,

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:38.630
which encapsulates
all of these elements

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:41.450
and does a lot of the
heavy lifting for you

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:44.920
so that you can quickly pull
together your authorization

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:46.770
step.

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:51.420
That library is available for
Android and iOS, and as I said,

00:25:51.420 --> 00:25:56.760
it supports OAuth2
and OpenID Connect.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:26:01.080
The Chrome Custom Tab
then is built into that

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.430
and it uses the shared
Chrome cookie jar.

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:07.340
But it will also fall
back to a default browser

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:11.460
if Chrome just happens
to not be available.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:15.690
So what that means is we can
completely simplify the SSO

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.470
offering, making
sure that users don't

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:21.830
have to be continually logging
in using their username

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:23.000
and password.

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:28.010
And you can also implement
more consistent session expiry

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:33.540
policies without causing
a really big UX headache.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:36.660
Don't forget though, we
need to enable the app

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:39.195
to accept that login
hint configuration.

00:26:39.195 --> 00:26:42.940
We're going to talk
about that in just a bit.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:46.960
So head on over to
GitHub.com/OpenID.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:49.210
There's also a video
here which goes

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:53.010
into a lot more detail over
the course of half an hour

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:56.590
where it will give you the
details of how to implement

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:59.650
that and some of the gory
details of how it really

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:01.720
works in the back end.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:06.570
So let's move on now to
talk about Chrome OS.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:09.620
So you've probably heard
already that Chrome OS now

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:14.000
has started supporting
Android apps for Google Play.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.100
What's actually happening
in the background

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:21.860
is Chrome OS includes now a
fully CTS compatible instance

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:22.940
of Android running.

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:24.650
And it's actually
Android Marshmallow

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:27.500
that's running
currently within Chrome.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:31.400
And for the most
part, apps just work.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.800
But of course, there's
a set of optimizations

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:36.380
that you can make to
make the best use out

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:38.690
of a Chrome device.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:42.210
And we'll go through
each of these.

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:45.890
So first of all, we've got to
ensure that it's compatible.

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:48.560
For the most part, it will be.

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:51.330
But there's a few things that
you've got to bear in mind.

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:56.240
First of all, Chromebooks don't
necessarily have a touchscreen.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.930
So the best way around this
is to make sure you explicitly

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:04.420
state in your manifest that
you don't need a touchscreen.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:08.840
But of course with a
trackpad or a mouse,

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:11.630
the Chrome device will
actually implement

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:13.490
what we call fake touch.

00:28:13.490 --> 00:28:17.340
So you still have that
similar kind of interaction.

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:21.660
And you should handle then
those differences at run time.

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:24.170
The other thing worth
pointing out here

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:27.590
is that things like GPS,
accelerometer, and gyro

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:29.620
are often also not included.

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:33.240
So don't require
those in your manifest

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:35.570
as a static requirement.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:39.310
Rather handle their absence,
if they are absent, at runtime.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:45.230
Of course on a Chrome
device, whether you've

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:47.940
got a laptop or a
large screen display,

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:51.720
you've got a lot more screen
real estate to play with.

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:55.470
Have a look at how do you
reflow your app, for example,

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:57.450
from what is probably
a single column

00:28:57.450 --> 00:29:00.360
layout on a smartphone
to a multiple column

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:02.940
layout on a much larger device.

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.770
What this means
is that your users

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.140
are able to use a
lot more of your apps

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:10.035
functionality without having to
scroll backwards and forwards.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:18.970
Also, things like opening menu
items or menus by default--

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:21.910
this means that your features
are much more discoverable.

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:25.920
Also you need far fewer
clicks for the user to access

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:29.970
that functionality

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:33.090
Chrome OS has
multiwindow support.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:37.140
The Android apps in
Chrome OS lay out

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:40.650
into various different
sizes at the moment.

00:29:40.650 --> 00:29:45.620
And very soon they'll be able
to do full variable window

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:47.160
scalability.

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.590
But for now, there
are three layouts.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.050
There's portrait and landscape.

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:58.530
And they're very much the aspect
ratio of a Nexus 7 or Nexus 9

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:03.020
device, and then of course
full window mode as well.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:08.200
What you need to do
is make sure that you

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:10.570
test your app in
these various modes,

00:30:10.570 --> 00:30:12.670
and particularly
the transitions,

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:17.050
to make sure that your app
is reflowing and looking nice

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:18.060
as it does so.

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:25.030
So let's talk a little bit
about keyboards and text entry.

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:28.170
Well, text entry and things like
point and click with a mouse

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:30.130
will just work.

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:33.030
But when your users have
got a physical keyboard

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:36.930
in front of them, they will
expect keyboard shortcuts

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.210
to work.

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:41.220
You should add
keyboard listeners

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:43.980
to either your
activities or your views

00:30:43.980 --> 00:30:48.360
in order to provide that kind
of functionality for your users.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:52.890
In this example, I've
taken one of my activities,

00:30:52.890 --> 00:31:00.600
and I've actually overladen
the onKeyUp method,

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:08.250
looking for Control-P. And
then I call my print method.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.110
Bear in mind though
that if there's

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:13.020
explicit key combinations
that you're not handling,

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:16.800
make sure that then you're
passing that onKeyUp

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.260
method back up to the superclass
in case that's handling it.

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:26.900
Chromebooks have started
coming with styluses.

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:30.500
For example, the new
Chromebook Plus and Chrome

00:31:30.500 --> 00:31:36.020
Pro from Samsung, those styluses
are not actually Bluetooth.

00:31:36.020 --> 00:31:37.970
They use a different technology.

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:41.480
But they behave in Android the
same way as a Bluetooth stylus,

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:45.680
and they're accessible
using the same methods.

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:50.510
So the way you handle these is
through handling motion events.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:56.430
You can use motion events
and their constants

00:31:56.430 --> 00:32:01.620
to actually filter for
the different actions.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:07.020
In this example, I'm explicitly
filtering for a stylus

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:11.600
and an erasure events.

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:16.970
What that means is I can be
felt doing particular actions

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:20.360
with a stylus at the same time
as having different actions

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:22.130
for finger touches.

00:32:22.130 --> 00:32:24.620
So you might, for
example, allow the user

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:29.090
to be drawing with the stylus,
but then panning and zooming

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:32.540
with the fingers, which actually
gives a very tactile kind

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:34.550
of user experience.

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:38.420
The styluses are double ended,
so you have a drawing end

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:40.640
and you have an erasing end.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:42.020
The other cool thing--

00:32:42.020 --> 00:32:46.520
and you'll see this in my draw
method at the bottom here--

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:49.910
is there's two other parameters
that come from a stylus--

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:51.950
the pressure that
the user is applying

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:55.940
to the screen, and also
the tilt of that stylus.

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:58.340
So you can get really,
really creative

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:01.910
with what you can do with things
like drawing and other input

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:04.610
techniques with that stylus
that you can't actually

00:33:04.610 --> 00:33:07.332
do with finger-based touch.

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:15.740
So let's now talk about
developing on managed Android.

00:33:15.740 --> 00:33:19.760
So over the last few
years, Google's introduced

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.660
a comprehensive suite of device
and app management features

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:26.660
into both Android
and Google Play.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:30.800
We're working together with 21
enterprise mobility management

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:34.460
partners, including
companies like AirWatch,

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:38.250
MobileIron BlackBerry, SOTI,
and of course Google Mobile

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:43.610
Management which comes
as part of G Suite.

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:46.460
There are two
management modes that I

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.690
want to discuss here today--

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:51.140
first of all, the
work profile, which

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:56.510
is predominately used in a bring
your own device or BYOD model.

00:33:56.510 --> 00:34:00.710
Here, work data
and personal data

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:05.340
are kept separate in two
separate Android profiles.

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:09.489
And apps and notifications that
are running in the work profile

00:34:09.489 --> 00:34:14.810
are then labeled with the
orange briefcase badge.

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:17.060
The fully managed
mode is typically

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:22.790
for devices which are
owned by the enterprise.

00:34:22.790 --> 00:34:25.969
And they give administrators
full visibility

00:34:25.969 --> 00:34:27.480
of what's going
on in the device,

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:31.010
including some deep inspection
techniques like process

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:34.389
logging, remote bug
reports, and forensics.

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:40.290
Across both of these
management modes,

00:34:40.290 --> 00:34:45.760
managed Google Play is
used to distribute apps.

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:50.800
We're particularly proud
of the work profile.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:54.489
In the work profile,
IT administrators

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:57.790
can be confident that
the data is secure.

00:34:57.790 --> 00:35:01.120
They can set policies
to ensure things

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.050
like copy-paste doesn't
work from within a work app

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:06.730
into a personal app.

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:10.150
So it really is like having
two separate devices.

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:12.670
At the same time,
the IT administrator

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:16.150
can be confident that they're
not coming into contact

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:18.370
with the user's personal data.

00:35:18.370 --> 00:35:20.890
This can be important
for compliance reasons,

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:24.010
particularly in
regions like the EU.

00:35:24.010 --> 00:35:27.100
On the user's side,
they can be confident

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:30.850
that their employer isn't
spying on their personal usage

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:33.100
of their own device.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:37.120
And if the user leaves
then, leaves the company,

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:40.300
the IT admin just
removes the work profile,

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:42.145
and the rest of the
device is left intact.

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:49.090
So from a development
standpoint then,

00:35:49.090 --> 00:35:51.100
the fully managed device
mode doesn't actually

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:52.840
present any additional hurdles.

00:35:52.840 --> 00:35:55.690
The apps just work
exactly the same way.

00:35:55.690 --> 00:35:57.520
But in the work profile,
there are a couple

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:00.220
of things that you should
be aware of to make sure

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:01.555
that your app supports.

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:08.380
So first of all,
handling intents,

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:12.120
and then secondly sharing
data with other apps--

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.540
so similar to the
copy-paste example,

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:21.810
an IT administrator can block
different kinds of intents.

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:24.930
And it's very difficult for you
as a developer to know up front

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:27.450
which intents are allowed
and which are blocked

00:36:27.450 --> 00:36:32.610
and which might be resolvable
within the work profile.

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:36.150
The best way to check that
is to call resolveActivity

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:39.840
for your intent before
you try and fire it.

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:41.910
And that means that if
it doesn't resolve--

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:44.430
and this method returns
null in that case--

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:46.410
then you can handle
that gracefully

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:48.555
rather than the intent
going into nowhere.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:58.150
On the sharing data side, don't
use file URIs for sharing data

00:36:58.150 --> 00:37:00.700
when you hand off
to another app,

00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:05.890
because file URIs are
not going to resolve

00:37:05.890 --> 00:37:08.800
in the other profile.

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:13.690
Instead, rather use content URIs
and grant explicit permission

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:17.980
to the destination app
to access that data.

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:20.650
You can find a lot more
detail at this QR code

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:22.374
if you're interested.

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:28.470
So I briefly mentioned
managed configuration before.

00:37:28.470 --> 00:37:30.510
Have you ever had the
experience of having

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:34.500
to configure a complex
application as a user,

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:38.190
something like a VPN client
or even something as simple

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:40.210
as a mail client?

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:42.510
Sometimes there's a series
of complex parameters

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:44.670
that the user might
have to enter.

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:48.330
And this can lead to
expensive support calls

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:50.310
if not done properly.

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:53.460
Managed configuration
allows you to set those

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:56.440
remotely and automatically.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:59.680
And it's supported for both
the fully managed device

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:02.620
and indeed the work profile.

00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:04.090
One thing to bear
in mind though is

00:38:04.090 --> 00:38:08.870
don't send credentials
using managed configuration.

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:10.740
So how does that work?

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.380
Well first of all,
we're going to set up

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:16.320
the schema for that
configuration within the app's

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:17.550
manifest.

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:19.830
So when you publish
it to Google Play,

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.080
that's then going
to read the manifest

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:24.690
and then make it available
to the enterprise mobility

00:38:24.690 --> 00:38:27.780
management server, which
can then present the user

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:30.540
interface to an IT
admin, who can then

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:35.010
send that configuration
down to the device.

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:38.030
So let's just see an
example of that in code.

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:40.310
And we're going to go
back to the SSO example

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:42.230
that we talked about
before, and see

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:47.760
how do we add that login hint
field into the configuration.

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:49.670
So as part of the
manifest, you can

00:38:49.670 --> 00:38:52.340
add a restrictions.xml file.

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:56.870
Actually the Android APIs
talk about app restrictions.

00:38:56.870 --> 00:39:00.170
But we talk more fully
about this feature

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:04.370
in managed configuration because
it isn't just about restricting

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:06.590
the behavior of your app.

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:09.980
Here you can see a
number of parameters

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:12.770
that we need to provide
for each data field.

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:17.182
And of course the login hint
here is going to be a string.

00:39:17.182 --> 00:39:18.890
Then of course you
need to get the values

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:21.320
within your app at runtime.

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:23.600
This is straightforward to do.

00:39:23.600 --> 00:39:26.330
You just need to call
the restriction service.

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:31.850
And then using the key, login
hint out of the key-value pair,

00:39:31.850 --> 00:39:36.080
you can get the value for that.

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:38.750
Also bear in mind that
the IT administrator

00:39:38.750 --> 00:39:42.380
might send new
values to that field

00:39:42.380 --> 00:39:44.540
while your app is running.

00:39:44.540 --> 00:39:48.260
So you should also make
sure you can handle updates.

00:39:48.260 --> 00:39:53.670
The way to do that is to
set up a broadcast receiver,

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:59.270
when will handle the broadcast
intent sent by the system

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:03.600
when new app configuration
comes down to the device.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:07.030
And then of course
register that receiver

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:09.680
to receive that broadcast.

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:11.540
The thing to bear
in mind here is

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:13.940
you can't do that
statically in the manifest.

00:40:13.940 --> 00:40:17.840
You have to do that
dynamically by registering

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:19.460
the receiver at runtime.

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:26.280
You should also be aware of a
industry organization called

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.830
the AppConfig Community.

00:40:28.830 --> 00:40:32.250
AppConfig was set up by some
leading enterprise mobility

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:33.570
management partners.

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:37.020
And they're working with
developers, mobile platforms,

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:39.450
and others in the
ecosystem to build out

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:45.600
common best practices to enable
apps and mobile management

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:47.790
to work well together.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:51.300
Google is working with
AppConfig to lay out

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.595
the Android guidance.

00:40:56.850 --> 00:41:01.080
There's also a special case
of fully managed devices

00:41:01.080 --> 00:41:04.770
which is for single use devices.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:09.510
Things like kiosks, ruggedized
devices with barcode scanners,

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:15.200
ePOS terminals, and
interfaces to large equipment,

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:19.400
all examples of
single use devices.

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:22.040
And in those cases,
you don't want the user

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.900
to be able to go back to
Android, the Launcher,

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:29.090
to Settings, and do
other things with it.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:33.590
You want to just work with the
single app in front of you.

00:41:33.590 --> 00:41:38.360
As of Android Marshmallow, we've
introduced a new capability

00:41:38.360 --> 00:41:41.100
called lock task mode.

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:44.200
In Lock Task mode,
the user can actually

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:47.170
escape the app that is
locked to the screen.

00:41:47.170 --> 00:41:50.380
You can also stop the device
from switching off the screen.

00:41:50.380 --> 00:41:54.900
So you've always got an
attractive display switched on.

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:58.020
Things like Home and
Recents button and Settings

00:41:58.020 --> 00:41:59.260
are all hidden.

00:41:59.260 --> 00:42:01.170
So the user can't
tamper with the device.

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:04.640
It really is locked down.

00:42:04.640 --> 00:42:06.830
So how do you
actually enable that?

00:42:06.830 --> 00:42:09.620
Well first of all, the
actual devices themselves

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:14.630
need to be under management,
so through an EMM or enterprise

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:16.500
mobility manager.

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:19.490
That EMM needs to
whitelist your app

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:23.820
with the system to make sure
that the app is authorized.

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:26.330
Then once that's done, it's
actually very straightforward

00:42:26.330 --> 00:42:29.390
to lock your app to the screen.

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:32.990
It's worth though checking
that your app is actually

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:34.040
whitelisted.

00:42:34.040 --> 00:42:37.340
So checking this
isLockTaskPermitted

00:42:37.340 --> 00:42:39.020
will actually check
that the EMM has

00:42:39.020 --> 00:42:41.630
been through that
whitelisting process.

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:45.086
And then you can invoke
the lock task mode.

00:42:45.086 --> 00:42:46.460
The thing to bear
in mind is when

00:42:46.460 --> 00:42:51.780
you're testing, make sure it's
easy to unlock the app again.

00:42:51.780 --> 00:42:54.830
Because we've seen it
time and time again,

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:58.490
developers locking the app to
the screen and then not being

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:01.460
able to unlock it without
rebooting and loading

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:02.995
the app back onto the device.

00:43:06.280 --> 00:43:08.650
So as an example
here, a company called

00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:14.170
Teem, formerly EventBoard, are
using this on Android devices.

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:18.160
Teem have a meeting
with management app,

00:43:18.160 --> 00:43:21.220
together with tablets
or kiosk devices,

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:24.820
to manage meeting room
bookings and other things

00:43:24.820 --> 00:43:27.070
around the meeting room.

00:43:27.070 --> 00:43:29.170
It's solely for
meeting management,

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:31.660
so they've locked
that app to the device

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:35.850
to avoid users from
tampering with anything else.

00:43:35.850 --> 00:43:40.720
Teem actually use a combination
of the single use APIs,

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:44.020
like lock task mode, together
with the Android Device

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:45.220
Management APIs.

00:43:45.220 --> 00:43:48.370
So they don't need to
use a third party EMM.

00:43:48.370 --> 00:43:52.390
They have the solution
end to end together.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:54.490
And Teem provides all
of that functionality

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:56.685
within their own
management portal.

00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:02.020
So let's move on
then to deploying

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:05.150
your apps into enterprises.

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:06.950
One of the big challenges
here, if you're

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:11.540
using a home built
enterprise app store

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:15.260
or going through
an EMM potentially,

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:21.140
is you have to enable unknown
sources in order to allow

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:23.780
those apps to be deployed.

00:44:23.780 --> 00:44:28.700
Unknown sources allows users
to download and install apps

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:33.440
from any source, not just
potentially the enterprise app

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:36.810
store that you want them to.

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:40.580
This can be a major
security risk.

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:44.230
Also, it is difficult
to push apps to devices

00:44:44.230 --> 00:44:46.720
without user interaction.

00:44:46.720 --> 00:44:48.850
Very often we've
seen that users have

00:44:48.850 --> 00:44:50.860
to accept that
push install, which

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:52.570
can be frustrating
for admins who

00:44:52.570 --> 00:44:56.500
are trying to get apps
to users very quickly.

00:44:56.500 --> 00:45:00.640
Also, there may be other apps--

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:03.070
for example, the
consumer Play Store--

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:06.730
which is there which is allowing
the user to install apps

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:09.430
in an uncontrolled way.

00:45:09.430 --> 00:45:14.800
So we've introduced management
capabilities within Google Play

00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:20.270
to provide an enterprise
app store for users.

00:45:20.270 --> 00:45:23.800
IT administrators are
able to curate the app

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:26.200
store for their
users, laying out

00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:29.230
those apps into
clusters and pages

00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:32.910
to enable easy discoverability.

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:36.400
The IT admin is also able
to whitelist explicitly

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:39.340
which apps are allowed
within the work space

00:45:39.340 --> 00:45:44.810
and which are not, ensuring
the security of any data

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:49.720
in the work profile or
fully managed device.

00:45:49.720 --> 00:45:52.600
But the question is, well why
really work with Google Play?

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:58.580
And this slide is
probably being seen

00:45:58.580 --> 00:46:00.860
at least-- this is the
fourth time, I think,

00:46:00.860 --> 00:46:02.450
during this conference.

00:46:02.450 --> 00:46:05.840
But this is really the
critical part of it.

00:46:05.840 --> 00:46:10.760
Google Play is a secure
source of applications.

00:46:10.760 --> 00:46:13.400
We've been collecting
data about potentially

00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:17.880
harmful applications for
the last two years or so.

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:20.420
And the data is really
quite remarkable.

00:46:20.420 --> 00:46:25.220
If you split that data on
potentially harmful application

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:30.680
infection rates by those devices
that are solely getting apps

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:33.590
from Google Play versus
those that are getting them

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:35.675
from other sources,
you see a factor

00:46:35.675 --> 00:46:39.440
of 20 difference in
the infection rate

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:43.730
from potentially harmful
applications or malware.

00:46:43.730 --> 00:46:47.180
And those results are very
consistent, as you can see.

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:50.270
So unknown sources really
indiscriminately opens up

00:46:50.270 --> 00:46:51.410
devices.

00:46:51.410 --> 00:46:55.790
And we really recommend using
Google Play as your enterprise

00:46:55.790 --> 00:47:00.560
app store, as well as
obviously it's a great consumer

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:02.390
store as well.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:04.040
So why else?

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:08.600
Well Google Play as a platform
has a number of big benefits.

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:10.490
Firstly, it's secure.

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:13.640
We're using strong
authentication

00:47:13.640 --> 00:47:16.910
with encrypted delivery of
those apps down to your device.

00:47:16.910 --> 00:47:18.830
And those apps are all signed.

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:24.560
And we're making sure
that any updates of apps

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:28.910
are signed by the same key
as the original app itself.

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:31.040
We have a global
server footprint.

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:34.970
Urs talked a lot about
that this morning.

00:47:34.970 --> 00:47:39.080
We use patch updates rather
than sending the whole APK down

00:47:39.080 --> 00:47:40.490
for every update.

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:42.380
We only send down
the differences

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:44.090
in a compressed format.

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:48.170
That significantly reduces
bandwidth requirement and also

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:49.790
battery.

00:47:49.790 --> 00:47:52.490
We do APK scanning
to making sure

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:56.420
that those APKs, your apps,
are free from vulnerabilities.

00:47:56.420 --> 00:47:59.330
I'll talk a little bit more
about that in the next slide.

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:01.820
We provide comprehensive
publishing tools.

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:04.410
I'm also going to talk more
about that in a minute.

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:06.950
Reporting tools,
things like seeing

00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:10.490
how quickly is the app being
installed out to users,

00:48:10.490 --> 00:48:12.670
crash reports, and so forth.

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:15.680
And updates are
deployed automatically

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:21.000
in a sensitive way to both
battery and bandwidth.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:24.110
On the management
side, we enable

00:48:24.110 --> 00:48:29.330
IT admins to curate that store,
as you saw in the screenshot.

00:48:29.330 --> 00:48:31.850
Push installs and
managed configuration

00:48:31.850 --> 00:48:33.380
we've already talked about.

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:37.010
And there's actually no
need to use Google accounts.

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:39.680
We've got the ability to
work with any identity

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:45.680
provider with Google Play
without having to create

00:48:45.680 --> 00:48:47.560
separate Google accounts.

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:49.930
And of course we've
got a full set of APIs

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:54.470
which your favorite
EMM can integrate into.

00:48:54.470 --> 00:48:58.080
So let's just quickly
look at that APK scanning.

00:48:58.080 --> 00:48:59.852
If you went to Ed's
talk earlier today,

00:48:59.852 --> 00:49:02.060
he would have talked a lot
more about this in detail.

00:49:05.800 --> 00:49:09.040
The thing for you to
consider though is you

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:12.460
don't necessarily know
what SDKs and what

00:49:12.460 --> 00:49:15.820
versions of those that
your dev teams are using.

00:49:15.820 --> 00:49:20.470
This isn't about scanning for
malicious intent necessarily.

00:49:20.470 --> 00:49:25.390
But it gives you an early heads
up to legacy versions of SDKs

00:49:25.390 --> 00:49:27.880
that might have vulnerabilities.

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:32.300
You remember all the PR around
the Heartbleed vulnerability

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:36.160
a couple of years ago where
legacy versions of SSL

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:38.950
were being used
across the internet,

00:49:38.950 --> 00:49:41.650
causing complete chaos.

00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:44.380
We are able to detect all
of these vulnerabilities

00:49:44.380 --> 00:49:46.930
on this slide today, and
we're adding to that list

00:49:46.930 --> 00:49:47.650
all the time.

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:53.620
Also about rollout management--

00:49:53.620 --> 00:49:56.530
Google Play now provides
a comprehensive suite

00:49:56.530 --> 00:50:01.420
of rollout tools, things
like alpha/beta testing.

00:50:01.420 --> 00:50:05.680
You can create groups of
users for early versions

00:50:05.680 --> 00:50:09.100
of your app, which will
then roll out automatically

00:50:09.100 --> 00:50:12.460
to those users using the same
update mechanisms that Google

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:15.370
Play uses for regular updates.

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:18.520
But that then protects
the rest of your user base

00:50:18.520 --> 00:50:21.430
on the stable
production version.

00:50:21.430 --> 00:50:25.510
Once your alpha/beta version
is ready for prime time,

00:50:25.510 --> 00:50:29.740
you can then use controlled
percentage rollouts

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:32.950
in order to slowly ramp
up the number of users

00:50:32.950 --> 00:50:34.660
that you have on that version.

00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:38.830
And you can hold that at any
time if you have problems.

00:50:38.830 --> 00:50:43.840
You can also publish
at a particular date.

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:48.820
We've also just launched a fully
managed app releases process

00:50:48.820 --> 00:50:51.220
where you can really define
a release at the beginning

00:50:51.220 --> 00:50:56.340
of that flow and then take
it down through alpha/beta

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:57.730
and production.

00:50:57.730 --> 00:51:01.770
And you can watch a great
video by a colleague of mine

00:51:01.770 --> 00:51:03.310
at this QR code.

00:51:06.080 --> 00:51:11.990
So if you are an ISV
or an agency developer,

00:51:11.990 --> 00:51:16.160
you can just start straight
away by registering

00:51:16.160 --> 00:51:19.630
for a Google Play Developer
account at this URL.

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:22.520
All you have to do
is pay a $25 sign

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:25.860
up fee, which is a one time fee.

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:30.020
If you're an IT department, you
need to be working with an EMM

00:51:30.020 --> 00:51:31.730
that we've partnered with.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:35.030
Here is a selection
of the 21 EMM partners

00:51:35.030 --> 00:51:39.410
that we're currently
integrated to.

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:41.280
I particularly
want to highlight,

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:43.980
since we're here
at Google Cloud,

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:46.830
how you set this
up with G Suite.

00:51:46.830 --> 00:51:49.950
So if you're not already using
Google Mobile Management,

00:51:49.950 --> 00:51:53.100
you'll see this little pop
up, Set up mobile management,

00:51:53.100 --> 00:51:56.370
in the front panel of the
administration console.

00:51:59.100 --> 00:52:04.590
Once you've signed up to that,
then within the administrator's

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:07.980
role-based access
set up, you can then

00:52:07.980 --> 00:52:10.350
create an administrator
with the ability

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:13.440
to upload private apps to Play.

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:15.444
If you want to
publish public apps,

00:52:15.444 --> 00:52:16.860
then there's nothing
else you need

00:52:16.860 --> 00:52:19.230
to do for your administrator.

00:52:19.230 --> 00:52:24.810
Then for that admin, sign up
to for same Google Play account

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:27.510
we saw in the previous slide.

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:32.100
And then if you want to set
up private applications, which

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:35.400
are restricted to your
business, then you'll

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:39.150
see this Restrict
distribution section,

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:40.770
which you can just check.

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:44.180
And actually this is an
example of, yeah, my company.

00:52:47.070 --> 00:52:50.660
For agencies, you can
get delegated access

00:52:50.660 --> 00:52:53.630
to your customer's
developer account

00:52:53.630 --> 00:52:55.760
so that you can
manage the publishing

00:52:55.760 --> 00:53:00.530
and update flows on their behalf
rather than having to send them

00:53:00.530 --> 00:53:04.010
APKs for them to upload.

00:53:04.010 --> 00:53:07.400
Google Play's role-based
access mechanism

00:53:07.400 --> 00:53:10.250
allows your customer
to then delegate

00:53:10.250 --> 00:53:14.540
access just for your apps
rather than them having

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:17.685
to be concerned potentially
with you interfering with apps

00:53:17.685 --> 00:53:19.060
that they're
publishing directly.

00:53:21.950 --> 00:53:26.430
So that was a lot of
content we've been through.

00:53:26.430 --> 00:53:29.820
But let me just try
and summarize that.

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:31.530
I think what we've
shown is that Google

00:53:31.530 --> 00:53:36.390
has a comprehensive set of
products, features, and tools

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:39.150
to really help you get started
with publishing Android

00:53:39.150 --> 00:53:41.220
apps in the enterprise.

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:44.910
Remember to carefully consider
your design to make sure

00:53:44.910 --> 00:53:50.100
that you really do get the best
out of your mobile deployments.

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:53.370
Don't just port your
apps from desktop.

00:53:53.370 --> 00:53:57.930
When you're building, build
with security in mind first.

00:53:57.930 --> 00:53:59.850
Get consistency
across your fleet

00:53:59.850 --> 00:54:02.010
by not only supporting
Android but also

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:04.890
optimizing for Chrome OS.

00:54:04.890 --> 00:54:08.970
And enable management to
have your app deployable

00:54:08.970 --> 00:54:11.650
in a largest number
of situations.

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:14.850
And then finally,
deploy using Google Play

00:54:14.850 --> 00:54:21.480
for secure, reliable, and
manageable deployment.

00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:22.980
I also want to
highlight that if you

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:25.990
want to sign up for
more information,

00:54:25.990 --> 00:54:29.880
please go to this link where
you can sign up for a newsletter

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:32.310
and further information
about everything

00:54:32.310 --> 00:54:35.850
we're doing in connected
work spaces and mobile.

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:39.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

